<GuideLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Nearly Headless CMS is a library for building your own headless CMS. You bring the storage, identity, and HTTP layer. The library gives you the CMS service."
  nextGuide={{ href: "/guides/defining-content/", label: "Define your content" }}
>
  <p>
    You do not install a CMS product. You compose one from services. The library exposes a single
    <code>Cms.Service</code> Effect service. Your app provides the layers it needs: where content
    lives, who is making a request, and how HTTP routes reach the CMS.
  </p>

  <h2>The smallest useful app</h2>
  <p>For local development, start with the in-memory layer:</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { ContentDefinition, Cms } from "nearly-headless-cms"
import { InMemory } from "nearly-headless-cms/layers"

const snapshot = ContentDefinition.compileSnapshot({
  definitionSpaceId: "my-site",
  snapshotId: "v1",
  definitions: [/* your content types */],
})

const cmsLayer = InMemory.cms({ snapshot })`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    That one layer already wires open authorization, anonymous identity, crypto identifiers, and
    process-local storage. You can swap any piece later without touching the CMS service itself.
  </p>

  <h2>What you provide vs what the library owns</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You provide:</strong> persistence, authorization, identity, identifier generation, HTTP transport, and any custom API operations.</li>
    <li><strong>The library owns:</strong> the CMS service — creating entries, querying, assets, definition snapshots, and history.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where the example apps fit</h2>
  <p>
    The Example CMS in this monorepo shows a full composition with filesystem storage, delivery and
    management APIs, and a React dashboard. This Public Blog is the other half: a static site that
    reads from the Headless API at build time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Follow the guides in order, or jump to <a href="/guides/providing-layers/">Providing layers</a>
    if you already know your storage backend.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
